    "updated_at": "2025-10-16T10:19:38Z",
    "body": "This is what I think the spec says happens in that second load:\n1. _fetch an external module script graph_ of `\"./parent_import_parse_error.mjs\"`\n1. _fetch a single module script_ of `\"./parent_import_parse_error.mjs\"`\n1. Do the actual network fetching, and parse the result.\n1. Now we have a module script M1 for `./parent_import_parse_error.mjs`, with no parse error and no error to rethrow\n1. _fetch the descendants of and link_ of M1\n1. M1._LoadImportedModules_()\n1. _HostLoadImportedModule_(`\"./import_parse_error.mjs\"`)\n1. _fetch a single imported module script_ of `\"./import_parse_error.mjs\"`\n1. _fetch a single module script_ of `\"./import_parse_error.mjs\"`\n1. Cache hit! We now have a module script M2 with no parse error and an error to rethrow\n1. _FinishLoadingImportedModule_(M2)\n1. _HostLoadImportedModule_(`\"./parse_error.mjs\"`)\n1. _fetch a single imported module script_ of `\"./parse_error.mjs\"`\n1. _fetch a single module script_ of `\"./parse_error.mjs\"`\n1. Cache hit! We now have a module script M3 with a parse error and an error to rethrow\n1. _FinishLoadingImportedModule_(M3's parse error)\n1. It rejects the promise returned by _LoadImportedModules_\n\nSo I don't think that there is any extra loading happening.\n\nI don't think checking if there is an error to rethrow would hurt, as it would just short-circuit some steps giving the same end result.",
